{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "phase_shift",
      "parent_relation": "The parent maps (L, E_nu, theta, Delta_m2) to P_e_mu through one vacuum phase difference determined only by the exact two-state dispersion and then damps that same phase by energy-resolution averaging.",
      "child_relation": "Refine the sharply defined-energy assumption by replacing the single monochromatic phase with a physically motivated phase average over the produced neutrino wave packet, so the effective oscillation phase and visibility acquire additional E_nu- and L-dependent reshaping beyond the parent's single central-phase description.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "L",
        "E_nu",
        "Delta_m2"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At fixed theta and Delta_m2, increasing L/E_nu no longer only produces the parent's damped cosine with one central phase; the extrema shift slightly and the envelope suppression becomes asymmetric across successive oscillation lobes because different energy components within the packet accumulate different exact phases."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "x_phase",
        "expression": "kappa_osc*L*(sqrt(E_nu**2 + Delta_m2) - E_nu)",
        "role": "central exact vacuum phase argument already used by the parent"
      },
      {
        "name": "eta_rel",
        "expression": "Delta_m2/E_nu**2",
        "role": "measure of departure from the ultrarelativistic narrow-phase regime"
      },
      {
        "name": "chi_coh",
        "expression": "kappa_osc*L*Delta_m2/(E_nu**2 + Delta_m2)**(3/2)",
        "role": "existing scale controlling sensitivity of the phase to energy spread"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when the neutrino is not perfectly monochromatic and the phase varies appreciably across the intrinsic energy packet, i.e. moderate to large L with finite chi_coh and especially larger eta_rel; it vanishes in the narrow-packet or small-phase-spread limit.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, varying L and E_nu at fixed theta and Delta_m2 reveals small lobe-dependent phase reshaping once chi_coh is not tiny; in holdout the same mechanism strengthens at larger L/E_nu, producing a systematic phase-and-envelope distortion rather than a simple global damping change.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the sharply defined-energy limit where the intrinsic packet width tends to zero, so the phase average collapses to evaluation at the central E_nu and only the existing parent expression remains.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model includes detector/source energy-resolution damping but still assumes the neutrino itself has a perfectly sharp energy, so all coherence loss and phase centering are tied to one imposed averaging form instead of the source wave-packet energy distribution.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The neutrino is ultrarelativistic and has a sharply defined energy.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Finite production wave-packet energy spread causes averaging of the exact vacuum phase over emitted energies, modifying both oscillation visibility and the effective phase centering.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "L",
      "E_nu",
      "Delta_m2",
      "theta",
      "kappa_osc"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the monochromatic-neutrino idealization already singled out in the baseline assumptions, without adding matter effects or extra flavors. The parent response is effectively controlled by one central phase x_phase plus one damping factor derived from energy sensitivity, so for fixed theta and Delta_m2 it predicts a single-phase oscillation pattern versus L/E_nu whose extrema locations are locked to that central phase. The child would instead average the exact phase over a finite emitted-energy packet, still using the same declared inputs, which changes the scored target mapping itself: at fixed theta and Delta_m2, varying L and E_nu while holding the packet model implicit produces not just reduced contrast but slight lobe-dependent phase shifts and nonuniform curvature of peaks/troughs. This is scientifically admissible when phase variation across the packet is not negligible, roughly tracked by finite chi_coh and enhanced away from the deep ultrarelativistic limit eta_rel << 1. The effect disappears continuously for a sharply defined energy, giving a clear parent-recovery limit. The contrast is a mapping change, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the predicted P_e_mu(L,E_nu,theta,Delta_m2) itself changes shape across the sampled coordinates."
  },
